The spacing between elements in an array significantly impacts the coverage patterns and grating lobe suppression in Ka-band satellite links. When elements are closely spaced, the array can achieve higher directivity and gain, leading to narrower beam widths and improved signal strength. However, this high directivity also creates strong side lobes, which can cause interference and degrade the performance of neighboring beams. Conversely, wider spacing allows for lower directivity but reduces the side lobe strength, thereby mitigating interference. This balance is critical for optimizing coverage and minimizing interference in Ka-band satellite communications.

