In multibeam satellite systems, interference mitigation techniques must adapt differently when handling tunneled versus non-tunneled traffic. Tunneled traffic involves interference between beams within the same satellite, while non-tunneled traffic includes interference between satellite beams and terrestrial systems. The challenge lies in the practical implementation and performance implications of these techniques. For tunneled traffic, interference mitigation techniques focus on optimizing beamforming to minimize cross-talk and maximize data rates. This often requires advanced algorithms and simulations to ensure optimal performance under various conditions. Conversely, for non-tunneled traffic, interference mitigation techniques must address the additional complexity introduced by terrestrial systems, such as adjusting link budgets and implementing diversity techniques to prevent interference. The cost in terms of performance and capacity is significant, as the presence of terrestrial systems can limit the achievable data rates and increase the complexity of network operations. Therefore, these techniques must be carefully designed and implemented to ensure effective interference mitigation in both scenarios, balancing the need for performance and capacity with the practical constraints of satellite systems.

