The trade-offs in power allocation between quasi-TEM and quasi-TE1 modes in multi-beam satellite systems are primarily determined by unequal scan losses, which affect signal quality and system capacity. Quasi-TEM modes typically have higher scan losses, requiring more power to maintain signal quality. In contrast, quasi-TE1 modes have lower scan losses, allowing for more efficient power allocation. This difference impacts performance across beam patterns and interference scenarios, as unequal losses lead to unequal power allocations.

### Impact on Interference Suppression
The effectiveness of interference suppression techniques like power control and beamforming is significantly influenced by scan losses. Higher scan losses in quasi-TEM modes necessitate more power to combat interference, potentially leading to increased power consumption and reduced system efficiency. In contrast, quasi-TE1 modes can manage interference more effectively with lower power requirements, resulting in more efficient power allocation and potentially better interference suppression.

### Performance Metrics
The scan losses impact key performance metrics such as bit error rate (BER), throughput, and latency. Higher scan losses in quasi-TEM modes can lead to degraded signal quality and increased BER, affecting the overall performance of the system. In contrast, quasi-TE1 modes may exhibit better performance metrics due to lower scan losses, potentially offering a more favorable trade-off between signal quality and power efficiency.

### System Design Considerations
System design considerations, including antenna aperture and satellite platform limitations, need to be adjusted to mitigate the impact of scan losses. For instance, in quasi-TEM modes, the antenna aperture may need to be larger or more reflective to compensate for higher scan losses, which can affect system design and cost. On the other hand, quasi-TE1 modes can benefit from more efficient power allocation, potentially reducing the need for large antennas, which can simplify system design and reduce costs.

### Comparison with Other Modes
The scan loss differences between quasi-TEM and quasi-TE1 modes compare favorably to other modes used in multi-beam satellite systems. For example, in quasi-TEM modes, higher scan losses can lead to reduced system capacity and increased power usage, which is not ideal for maximizing system performance. In contrast, quasi-TE1 modes offer a more balanced approach, providing better signal quality and system capacity with lower power requirements, making them a preferable choice for many applications.

